\section{Evaluation protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

\paragraph{Data.}
We use the public MTEB results~\citep{muennighoff2023mteb} at one pinned commit of the results repository (Section~\ref{sec:release}). A benchmark is one text task; a cell is a model's main score on it, in points on a 0--100 scale. We keep one revision per model and the modal dataset revision and language set per task, drop configuration variants and duplicate model names, and keep tasks with at least 50 models and models with at least 30 tasks, iterated to a fixed point. The result is a matrix of $M=238$ models and $N=328$ tasks with 45{,}775 observed cells (41\% missing) and 83 organisations (Table~\ref{tab:coverage}). Of the tasks, 117 are retrieval and 73 classification: MTEB tasks are fine-grained, so $N$ is large relative to the number of task types. The coverage thresholds were chosen after viewing counts of models and tasks, not scores.

\paragraph{Held-out units and splits.}
The unit of hold-out is the organisation: models from one organisation are fitted together and held out together, since a lab's models share training data and recipes and a split by model would let a sibling stand in for the new model. The 83 organisations are assigned to 10 folds, and every model is held out once, with standardisation, factor model, rank, and margins fitted on the other folds only (the group split, G). Two further splits stress the setting: a temporal split trains on the 70\% earliest-released dated models and tests on the later ones (T; 68 test models from 37 organisations), and a strength split trains on the weakest 75\% by an additive model offset and tests on the strongest quarter (S; 60 test models from 32 organisations). Organisations overlap between train and test in T.

\paragraph{Uncertainty.}
Every curve is the mean over held-out models of the model's own RMSE, in raw points unless stated. Intervals are 90\% group-bootstrap intervals (5000 resamples of held-out organisations, each model weighted equally). Random selection (S0) is the mean over 50 seeded draws per model and $k$.

\paragraph{Margins and verdicts.}
Whether one selection rule is better than another depends on how small a difference is worth caring about. For benchmark $j$ let $g_j$ be the median score gap between adjacent training models and $f_j$ the error of predicting a model's score on $j$ from all its other scores. The margin is $\delta_j=\min(0.5\,g_j,\,0.25\,f_j)$ and $\delta$ is its mean over the evaluated target cells, computed on training models only. For the paired difference $d_k=\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{S0}}-\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{S6}}$ at each $k$ with its 90\% interval $[l,h]$ the verdict is: \emph{design helps} if $l>\delta$; \emph{equivalent} if $-\delta<l$ and $h<\delta$; \emph{random better} if $h<-\delta$; otherwise \emph{undecided}, which we report as such and never as a tie. These margins were written down before any error was computed. They turn out to be small ($\delta\approx0.10$ points against an all-others floor of about 4.6 points, because adjacent MTEB models are close), so we also report the verdict at a second margin of $0.25\,f_j$ (about 1.2 points), declared before the contrast was computed.

\paragraph{How many benchmarks.}
Because $\delta\le0.25 f_j$, an error of at most $\delta$ is unreachable. We therefore define the needed run-set size on the excess over the model's own floor: $k^*(\epsilon)$ is the smallest $k$ on the grid $\{1,2,3,5,8,12,20,32,50,80,109\}$ at which the mean excess of the model's RMSE over its own all-others floor, on the same target cells, is at most $\epsilon$, for $\epsilon\in\{\delta,2\delta,0.25 f,0.5 f\}$; its interval is the smallest such $k$ per bootstrap resample. The grid ends at $\lfloor N/3\rfloor=109$.
