4	\paragraph{Setting.}
5	A historical matrix $Y\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times N}$ of scores of $M$ models on $N$ benchmarks has observed entries $\Omega$; the rest are missing. A new model has a run set $R\subseteq[N]$ of benchmarks it could be run on. The evaluator picks $S\subseteq R$ with $|S|=k$, runs it, and predicts the model's scores on the other benchmarks from $y_S$ and the historical matrix. Everything below is fitted on the historical models only. Scores are standardised per benchmark with the historical mean and standard deviation; errors are reported after the inverse transform, in the benchmark's own points.
6	
7	\paragraph{Inference model.}
8	We use a probabilistic factor model with missing data~\citep{tipping1999ppca},
9	\begin{equation}
10	y_{mj}=\mu_j+v_j^{\top}z_m+\varepsilon_{mj},\qquad z_m\sim\mathcal N(0,I_r),\quad \varepsilon_{mj}\sim\mathcal N(0,\psi_j),
11	\end{equation}
12	with $\mu_j,v_j\in\mathbb{R}^r,\psi_j$ estimated by EM on the observed entries. For a new model with revealed scores $y_S$ the posterior of its latent vector is Gaussian,
13	\begin{equation}
14	\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)=\Bigl(I_r+\sum_{j\in S}\psi_j^{-1}v_jv_j^{\top}\Bigr)^{-1},\qquad
15	\hat z=\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)\sum_{j\in S}\psi_j^{-1}v_j\,(y_j-\mu_j),
16	\end{equation}
17	and the prediction for any benchmark $j'$ is $\mu_{j'}+v_{j'}^{\top}\hat z$. The rank $r$ is chosen by inner cross-validation over groups of training models, at the same number of revealed scores $k$ at which the model is evaluated. We write P5 for this inference rule; the other inference rules we compare against are listed in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.
18	
19	\paragraph{Selection.}
20	$\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)$ does not depend on the revealed scores, so selection is an offline subset problem. Given a target set $T$ of benchmarks to be predicted, the expected posterior variance of the predictions is
21	$F_T(S)=\sum_{j\in T}v_j^{\top}\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)\,v_j=\operatorname{tr}\bigl(V_T\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)V_T^{\top}\bigr)$, a Bayesian A-optimal design criterion~\citep{chaloner1995bayesian,mulder2009multidimensional}. We minimise it greedily over $S\subseteq R$, adding the benchmark with the largest decrease; each gain is a rank-one update (Sherman--Morrison). We write S6 for this rule. Greedy selection carries a guarantee through the submodularity ratio of the objective~\citep{das2011submodular,bian2017guarantees}, but the guarantee can be vacuous when noise is small, so we also compare greedy with exhaustive and swap search where those are feasible (Appendix). The rule is classical; we do not claim it as a contribution.
