\section{Error decomposition and the shape of the curve}
\label{sec:theory}

This section gives what the factor model implies for the error of a run set, so that the measured error in Section~\ref{sec:experiments} can be attributed. Throughout, $\psi_j$, $v_j$ and $\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}$ are as in Section~\ref{sec:method}, and parameters are first taken as known.

\begin{proposition}[Risk of a run set]\label{prop:t1}
If a new model follows the factor model and benchmark $j\notin S$ is predicted by the posterior mean given $y_S$, its expected squared error is
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\,(y_j-\hat y_j)^2=\psi_j+v_j^{\top}\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)\,v_j .
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\noindent\emph{Proof.} $y_j-\hat y_j=v_j^{\top}(z-\hat z)+\varepsilon_j$, where $z-\hat z\sim\mathcal N(0,\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S))$ is independent of $\varepsilon_j$ for $j\notin S$. \qed

Averaging over a target set $T$ gives $\overline\psi_T+D_T(S)$ with $D_T(S)=|T|^{-1}F_T(S)$, the design term that S6 minimises. The term is the same for any selection that gives the same $\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}$, it depends on the run set only through $\sum_{j\in S}\psi_j^{-1}v_jv_j^{\top}$, and it is non-increasing in $S$. Let $F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S)=\overline\psi_T+D_T(S)$ be the prediction of the mean squared error for the fitted parameters, and let $\mathrm{MSE}_T(S)$ and $\mathrm{MSE}_T(C)$ be the measured error with run set $S$ and with all of the pool revealed. The measured error then splits exactly into four terms,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{MSE}_T(S)=\underbrace{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)}_{\text{noise}}+\underbrace{\mathrm{MSE}_T(C)-F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)}_{\text{floor misfit}}+\underbrace{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S)-F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)}_{\text{design}}+\underbrace{\mathrm{rest}(S)}_{\text{estimation and misspecification}},
\label{eq:decomp}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{rest}(S)$ is whatever is left. With the parameters estimated from $M$ models rather than known, the extra error from estimating $V$ and $\psi$ is of order $1/M$ per model; the remainder in (\ref{eq:decomp}) also holds the error that comes from the model being wrong (non-linear links, heavy tails, correlated residuals within a task type). Section~\ref{sec:experiments} separates the two with data simulated from the fitted model.

\paragraph{Shape of the curve.}
Let $\tilde V_S$ be the rows $v_j/\sqrt{\psi_j}$, $j\in S$. Since $\Sigma_{\mathrm{post}}(S)=(I+\tilde V_S^{\top}\tilde V_S)^{-1}\preceq\bigl(1+\lambda_{\min}(\tilde V_S^{\top}\tilde V_S)\bigr)^{-1}I$,
\begin{equation}
D_T(S)\le\frac{\overline{\|v\|^2}_T}{1+\lambda_{\min}(\tilde V_S^{\top}\tilde V_S)} .
\end{equation}
The bound separates two regimes. If $S$ cannot span the $r$ factors, $\lambda_{\min}=0$ and $D_T$ stays near $\overline{\|v\|^2}_T$ (it falls only along the directions the run set covers, so a few well-chosen benchmarks help most). Once $S$ spans them, the bound falls as $1/(1+ck)$ whenever $\lambda_{\min}$ grows at least as fast as $ck$, which holds for a random run set when the loadings are spread out rather than concentrated on a few benchmarks. With separated factors (eigenvalues well above $\psi$) the transition is a knee near the number of factors; with a spectrum that decays slowly it is a gradual closure, and the constant depends on how spread out the loadings are. Both regimes are tested on synthetic data in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.

The needed run-set size follows from the excess over the floor in RMSE, which is the quantity of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}: the model predicts
\begin{equation}
\text{excess}(k)=\sqrt{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S_k)+\text{floor misfit}}-\sqrt{\mathrm{MSE}_T(C)},
\end{equation}
taking the floor misfit as part of the floor, and $k^*_{\mathrm{pred}}(\epsilon)$ is the smallest $k$ at which this is at most $\epsilon$. This is a prediction from the training spectrum and noise alone, and we test it against the measured $k^*(\epsilon)$ rather than assume it.

